  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence for impacts from the coastal storm \ntonight into Friday are still decreasing. However, uncertainty \nstill exists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nModels are in better agreement regarding a more southerly track\nfor the coastal low, which will diminish potential impacts\nacross the forecast area. Low pressure will move from the \nMid-Atlantic region toward southern New England today. The low\nwill track south of the Gulf of Maine tonight, with high\npressure centered northwest of Maine. The northern edge of the \nprecipitation shield will reach Downeast areas tonight, though\namounts are still uncertain. Precipitation should start in the \nform of a rain/snow mix, then generally transition to mostly \nlight snow overnight. However, a rain/snow mix could persist \nalong the immediate Downeast coast. Snow accumulations across \nDowneast areas are generally expected to be around a half inch \nor less overnight. The system exits southeast of Nova Scotia \nFriday, though how rapidly precipitation diminishes is \nuncertain. Additional minor snow accumulations are possible \nearly Friday before precipitation diminishes and transitions \nback to rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is the potential for snow showers across \nthe North Friday night and Saturday, with accumulation possible \nacross higher elevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA closed upper level low sinks south into Northern Maine\nFriday night and Saturday. This will bring isolated to \nscattered snow showers (rain and snow showers NE Aroostook/N \nPenobscot Saturday afternoon) across the North. There is a \nchance for accumulating snow across the higher elevations, most \nlikely only a few tenths of an inch, however a few stronger \nsnow showers producing a quick inch or two of snow at elevations\nabove 1000 ft cannot be completely ruled out (20% or less \nchance). If this occurs, travel could be locally hazardous \nthere.",
